<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florida Keys</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> Keys are a chain of coral islands curving south-west from the southern tip of Florida for some 200 kilometres to Key West. Before European contact they were inhabited by peoples linked to the Calusa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tequesta">Tequesta</a>, and in the 18th century the last Calusa survivors took refuge there before leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. They are now joined to the mainland by the Overseas Highway.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florida-keys.jpg" alt="Florida Keys" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shanbin Zhao · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:U.S._Route_1,_Florida_Keys.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Florida Keys are a coral cay archipelago forming the southernmost part of the continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. They begin about 24 kilometres south of Miami and extend in an arc south-southwest and then westward to Key West, the westernmost of the inhabited islands, and on to the uninhabited Dry Tortugas. The islands divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> and define one edge of Florida Bay. The southern part of Key West is 150 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. The total land area is 356 square kilometres.</p><p>The northern and central keys are the exposed portions of an ancient coral reef, the Key Largo Limestone, which formed when sea levels stood about 7.6 metres above the present level, beginning some 130,000 years ago. The islands from Big Pine Key to the Marquesas Keys are exposed Miami Limestone. Just offshore lies the Florida Reef, which extends 270 kilometres. The climate is closer to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> than to the rest of Florida.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Keys were originally inhabited by the Calusa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tequesta">Tequesta</a> peoples and were charted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> in 1513. He named the islands Los Martires, the Martyrs, because from a distance they looked like suffering men. The word key comes from the Spanish cayo, meaning small island. For many years Key West was the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and grew prosperous on revenue from wrecking; it was well placed for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> and lay on the main trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. Improved navigation led to fewer shipwrecks, and Key West declined in the late 19th century.</p><p>The Keys were long reachable only by water. Henry Flagler's Overseas Railway was completed in the early 1910s after hurricanes disrupted the work in 1906, 1909 and 1910. On Labor Day, 2 September 1935, the strongest hurricane to strike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> made landfall near Islamorada, with a storm surge more than 5.3 metres high. More than 400 people were killed, including more than 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> veterans working on the roadway, whose evacuation train failed to reach them. The storm ended the railway, and U.S. Highway 1 replaced it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Overseas Highway, a 204-kilometre section of U.S. 1, runs over most of the inhabited islands, in places on converted railway bridges. The Seven Mile Bridge, built of concrete on pilings, connects Knight's Key to Little Duck Key. Key West is the county seat of Monroe County, and the major industries are fishing and tourism. Many Cubans settled in the Keys after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a>. The waters around the Keys form the Florida Keys National Marine Sanctuary, and Dry Tortugas National Park lies about 110 kilometres west of Key West.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Florida Keys” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Survivors fled to the <b>Keys</b> and to Cuba, the last leaving in 1760.</blockquote><a href="/state/calusa">Calusa</a></li></ul></section>
